  "studentQuestionsAI": {
    "headline": "Courses dominate site searches; career and research content draws far less traffic than expected.",
    "summary": "Between August 16 and November 14, 2026, the site drew nearly 1.26 million visits, and roughly 895,000 of those visitors were arriving for the first time. Among people who used the site's internal search, course-related terms accounted for 15.7 percent of searches, while careers accounted for 1.3 percent and research just 0.9 percent?suggesting that high-priority recruiting topics are not yet pulling students into the site through search.",
    "questions": [
      {
        "n": 1,
        "insight": "Careers, jobs, and internships made up only 1.3 percent of internal searches, which likely reflects thin content on outcomes rather than low student interest?departments supplying graduate placement and career destination data would give this topic more substance to surface."
      },
      {
        "n": 2,
        "insight": "Majors, minors, and programs accounted for 5.3 percent of internal searches, with public health, zoology, and neuroscience among the top terms, pointing to specific programs where prospective students are actively looking for information."
      },
      {
        "n": 3,
        "insight": "Advising and course permissions drew 4.6 percent of internal searches, with readmission appearing as the top term, suggesting students are navigating consequential academic transitions and need clear pathways to the right advisor."
      },
      {
        "n": 4,
        "insight": "Research terms made up just 0.9 percent of internal searches, though specific entries like 'REU,' 'psychology research,' and 'bridges lab' show that some students arrive with concrete programs in mind and need pages that match those terms."
      },
      {
        "n": 5,
        "insight": "Graduate-related terms do not appear among the recorded search topics for this period, which makes it difficult to assess demand?departments sharing funding details, application deadlines, and graduate placement records would create pages that give this question a presence in search."
      }
